## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests the ability to extend classic path DP problems with an additional resource constraint, evaluating proficiency in managing 3D DP states, path reconstruction via backtracking, and space optimization.

## Key Concepts
- 3D DP state (i, j, t) tracking exact t boosts used at position (i,j)
- Two transition options: boost or skip the current cell
- Path reconstruction by backtracking through the DP table
- Space optimization: reduce to two rows or maintain a compact parent pointer table

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithBoosts(grid, k, B) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: m }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(NEG))
  );
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  if (k > 0) dp[0][0][1] = grid[0][0] + B;

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (i > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t] !== NEG) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i-1][j][t] + grid[i][j]);
        }
        if (j > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t] !== NEG) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t] + grid[i][j]);
        }
        if (t > 0) {
          if (i > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t-1] !== NEG) {
            dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i-1][j][t-1] + grid[i][j] + B);
          }
          if (j > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t-1] !== NEG) {
            dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t-1] + grid[i][j] + B);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const boosts = [];
  let i = n - 1, j = m - 1, t = k;
  while (i > 0 || j > 0) {
    const fromTop = i > 0 && dp[i][j][t] === dp[i-1][j][t] + grid[i][j];
    const fromLeft = j > 0 && dp[i][j][t] === dp[i][j-1][t] + grid[i][j];
    const boostedFromTop = i > 0 && t > 0 && dp[i][j][t] === dp[i-1][j][t-1] + grid[i][j] + B;
    const boostedFromLeft = j > 0 && t > 0 && dp[i][j][t] === dp[i][j-1][t-1] + grid[i][j] + B;

    if (boostedFromTop || boostedFromLeft) boosts.push([i, j]);
    if (boostedFromTop || (i > 0 && fromTop && !boostedFromTop)) { i--; }
    else if (j > 0) { j--; if (!boostedFromLeft && !fromLeft) t--; }
  }

  return { maxSum: dp[n-1][m-1][k], boosts: boosts.reverse() };
}
```

## Complexity
- **Time:** O(n × m × k)
- **Space:** O(n × m × k) or O(m × k) with row optimization

## Why This Is Asked
DP over subsequences with a fixed length captures real-world problems like quantizing sequences while minimizing total variation; it tests state definition, reconstruction, and complexity trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- DP state: last index and length; - Transition: O(n) over prior indices; - Reconstruction via parent pointers; - Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSubsequence(A, k) {
  const n = A.length, INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][1] = 0;
  for (let t=2;t<=k;t++) {
    for (let i=0;i<n;i++) {
      for (let j=0;j<i;j++) {
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + Math.abs(A[i]-A[j]);
        if (val < dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = INF, last = -1;
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) if (dp[i][k] < best) { best = dp[i][k]; last = i; }
  const idx = Array(k);
  let t = k, cur = last;
  while (t>=1) { idx[t-1] = cur; cur = par[cur][t]; t--; }
  return {cost: best, indices: idx};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests dynamic programming on partitioning a string into numeric substrings, including numeric parsing and reconstruction, a common interview pattern.

## Key Concepts

- DP on string partitions with exact k blocks
- Efficient substring value computation and leading-zero handling
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  // val[i][j] = value of s[i..j-1]
  const val = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(n+1).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (s[i] === '0') { val[i][i+1] = 0; continue; }
    let cur = 0;
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      cur = cur*10 + (s.charCodeAt(j) - 48);
      val[i][j+1] = cur;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = val[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = t-1; j < i; j++) {
        if (s[j] === '0' && i - j > 1) continue;
        const cand = dp[j][t-1] + val[j][i];
        if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; par[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct cuts
  const cuts = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = par[i][t];
    cuts.push(i);
    i = j;
    t--;
  }
  cuts.push(0);
  cuts.reverse();
  return { sum: dp[n][k], cuts };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to allow at most k blocks instead of exactly k?
- How would you optimize for very large n or larger k (space/time optimizations, early pruning)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP on partitioning with a minimax objective, a practical budgeting scenario.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions; - Recurrence: min over j of max of subproblem and block sum; - Reconstruction via parent pointers.

## Code Example
```javascript
// sketch
function partitionMinMaxSum(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+a[i];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n},()=>new Array(k+1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n},()=>new Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][1]=pref[i+1];
  for (let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for (let i=0;i<n;i++){
      for (let j=0;j<i;j++){
        const val = Math.max(dp[j][t-1], pref[i+1]-pref[j+0]);
        if (val<dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; par[i][t]=j; }
      }
    }
  }
  return {best: dp[n-1][Math.min(k,n)], cuts: par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement a monotone-queue or divide-and-conquer optimization here?
- How would you adapt for exactly k vs at most k blocks and output exact cut points?

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates DP on selecting exact number of non-overlapping subarrays with reconstruction, common in profit/segmentation tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping subarrays
- Exact k selections
- Prefix sums for efficient range sums
- Backpointers for reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k){
  const n = A.length; const pre = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) pre[i] = pre[i-1]+A[i-1];
  const neg inf = -1e18;
  let dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> new Array(k+1).fill(neg_inf));
  let best = new Array(k+1).fill(neg_inf);
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++) best[t] = neg_inf;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      best[t] = Math.max(best[t], dp[i-1][t-1] - pre[i-1]);
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t], pre[i] + best[t]);
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

