- How would you adapt for at-most-k windows instead of exactly k?
- How would you reconstruct the actual windows efficiently and handle negative values?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over partitioning with a non-trivial segment value (max letter value).

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions with exact k segments
- Precomputing segment values (maxChar)
- Reconstructing the cut points

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPartitionValue(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const val = [...s].map(c => c.charCodeAt(0) - 96); // a=1..z=26
  const maxChar = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(n+1).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    let m=0;
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){
      m = Math.max(m, val[j]);
      maxChar[i][j+1] = m;
    }
  }
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const pre = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=Math.min(i,k);t++){
      for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
        const cand = dp[p][t-1] + maxChar[p][i];
        if(cand > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = cand; pre[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {value: dp[n][k], cuts: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle k=1 or k=n in constant time?
- Can you reduce space to O(nk) without losing reconstruction capabilities?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to turn path-based objectives into simple local transitions without extraneous state. The incremental gain depends only on adjacent cell values, enabling a clean DP.

## Key Concepts
- Grid DP with monotone moves
- Backpointers for path reconstruction
- Space optimization potential

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxAscents(grid){
  const n=grid.length, m=grid[0].length;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(m).fill(0));
  const par=Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(m).fill(null));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let j=0;j<m;j++){
      if(i===0 && j===0) continue;
      let top=-Infinity, left=-Infinity;
      if(i>0) top = dp[i-1][j] + (grid[i-1][j] < grid[i][j] ? 1:0);
      if(j>0) left = dp[i][j-1] + (grid[i][j-1] < grid[i][j] ? 1:0);
      if(top >= left){ dp[i][j] = top; par[i][j] = 'U'; } else { dp[i][j] = left; par[i][j] = 'L'; }
    }
  }
  // backtrack to build path
  return {score: dp[n-1][m-1], path: []};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
New angle: fixed window size with non-overlap, maximizing sum of window maxima.

## Key Concepts
- Precompute windowMax to make transitions O(1).
- Prefix DP with non-overlapping constraint.
- Reconstruction via a take/parent table.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumKWindows(A, n, k, m){
  const windowMax = Array(n+1).fill(-Infinity);
  for(let i=m; i<=n; i++){ // window ending at i
    let mx = -Infinity;
    for(let j=i-m+1; j<=i; j++) mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
    windowMax[i] = mx;
  }
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for(let i=0; i<=n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for(let t=1; t<=k; t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      if(i>=m && dp[i-m][t-1] + windowMax[i] > dp[i][t]){
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-m][t-1] + windowMax[i];
        take[i][t] = true;
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This classic problem evaluates your ability to design clean DP states for interval selection with reconstruction requirements. It strikes the right balance—accessible to beginners while effectively testing state structuring and backtracking skills.

## Key Concepts
- **dp[i][t]** manages selections across the first i elements with at most t subarrays
- **local[i][t]** distinguishes between extending an existing subarray and starting a new one
- Backtracking through the DP table to reconstruct segment boundaries

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumSubarrays(nums, k) {
    if (!nums.length || k <= 0) return { maxSum: 0, subarrays: [] };

    const n = nums.length;
    const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    const local = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    const endIdx = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));

    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(k, i); t++) {
            local[i][t] = Math.max(
                local[i - 1][t] + nums[i - 1],
                dp[i - 1][t - 1] + nums[i - 1]
            );
            if (local[i][t] === dp[i - 1][t - 1] + nums[i - 1]) {
                endIdx[i][t] = i - 1;
            }
            dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t] >= local[i][t] ? dp[i - 1][t] : local[i][t];
        }
    }

    const subarrays = [];
    let j = n, t = k;
    while (t > 0 && j > 0) {
        if (endIdx[j][t] !== -1) {
            let start = endIdx[j][t];
            while (start > 0 && endIdx[start][t] !== -1) start--;
            subarrays.unshift([start, endIdx[j][t]]);
            j = start;
            t--;
        } else {
            break;
        }
    }

    return { maxSum: dp[n][k], subarrays };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP formulation for selecting fixed-count non-overlapping subarrays with a per-block cost, plus reconstruction of blocks.

## Key Concepts

- DP over i (prefix) and t (blocks)
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- Running best to avoid O(n^2)
- Reconstructing start/end indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarraysWithCost(A, k, c){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const from = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  const bestVal = new Array(k+1).fill(NEG);
  const bestIdx = new Array(k+1).fill(-1);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++){
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      from[i][t] = {type:'skip', at:i-1, t};
    }
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++){
      const val = dp[i-1][t-1] - pref[i-1];
      if (val > bestVal[t]) { bestVal[t] = val; bestIdx[t] = i-1; }
      const cand = bestVal[t] + pref[i] - c;
      if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = cand;
        from[i][t] = {type:'end', end:i-1, start:bestIdx[t], t};
      }
    }
  }
  let i = n, t = k;
  const blocks = [];
  while (i > 0 && t > 0){
    const info = from[i][t];
    if (!info) break;
    if (info.type === 'end'){
      blocks.push([info.start, info.end]);
      i = info.start;
      t--;
    } else {
      i = info.at;
    }
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for at-most-k blocks?
- How would you handle negative values of c or allow zero-cost blocks?
- What modifications optimize space to O(k) while preserving reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests decoupling of interval boundaries from inner optimization and handling exact partitioning with mandatory gaps, mirroring scheduling patterns with local windows.

## Key Concepts
- Precompute best[l][r] = max subarray sum in A[l..r] using Kadane per start
- DP over i, t with a gap constraint: previous end <= l-2
- Reconstruct blocks by storing choices (l, i) for each t
- Space/time trade-offs; discuss rolling arrays and memory reuse

## Code Example
```javascript
// precompute best[l][r]
for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
  let cur = 0;
  for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
    cur = Math.max(cur + A[r], A[r]);
    best[l] = best[l] || [];
    best[l][r] = Math.max(best[l][r-1] ?? -Infinity, cur);
  }
}

// DP (conceptual)
for (let i = 0; i < n; i++)
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    dp[i][t] = max over l<=i of dp[l-2][t-1] + best[l][i];
  }
```

